## Dependency

`spring-boot-starter-web` pulls in `spring-boot-starter-tomcat` which includes:
- `tomcat-embed-core`
- `tomcat-embed-el`
- `tomcat-embed-websocket`

## Startup Flow

```
SpringApplication.run()
  → AutoConfiguration discovers TomcatServletWebServerFactory
  → Creates and configures Tomcat instance
  → Registers DispatcherServlet
  → Tomcat starts → binds to server.port
  → Application context refreshed
  → Application ready
```

## Customising Tomcat

```java
@Bean
public WebServerFactoryCustomizer<TomcatServletWebServerFactory> tomcatCustomizer() {
    return factory -> {
        factory.addConnectorCustomizers(connector -> {
            connector.setMaxPostSize(10 * 1024 * 1024); // 10MB
        });
        factory.addContextCustomizers(context -> {
            context.setSessionTimeout(30, TimeUnit.MINUTES);
        });
    };
}
```

## Tomcat Access Log

```properties
server.tomcat.accesslog.enabled=true
server.tomcat.accesslog.pattern=%h %l %u %t "%r" %s %b
server.tomcat.accesslog.directory=/var/log/app
```

## Production Reality

For high-traffic applications, consider tuning `server.tomcat.threads.max` and `server.tomcat.max-connections` rather than switching to a different server. Switching from Tomcat to Undertow for raw performance is sometimes beneficial, but the difference is marginal compared to application-level optimizations like connection pool tuning and query optimization.

## Generic Customizer

```java
@Component
public class ServerCustomizer implements WebServerFactoryCustomizer<ConfigurableWebServerFactory> {
    @Override
    public void customize(ConfigurableWebServerFactory factory) {
        factory.setPort(9090);
        factory.setAddress(InetAddress.getLoopbackAddress());
    }
}
```

## Tomcat-Specific Customizer

```java
@Component
public class TomcatCustomizer implements WebServerFactoryCustomizer<TomcatServletWebServerFactory> {
    @Override
    public void customize(TomcatServletWebServerFactory factory) {
        // Add an HTTP connector when app runs HTTPS
        factory.addAdditionalTomcatConnectors(createHttpConnector());

        // Add access log valve
        AccessLogValve valve = new AccessLogValve();
        valve.setDirectory("/var/log/app");
        valve.setPattern("%h %l %u %t \"%r\" %s %b %D");
        factory.addEngineValves(valve);
    }

    private Connector createHttpConnector() {
        Connector connector = new Connector("org.apache.coyote.http11.Http11NioProtocol");
        connector.setScheme("http");
        connector.setPort(8080);
        connector.setSecure(false);
        connector.setRedirectPort(8443);
        return connector;
    }
}
```

## @Bean Alternative

```java
@Bean
public WebServerFactoryCustomizer<TomcatServletWebServerFactory> tomcatCustomizer() {
    return factory -> factory.addConnectorCustomizers(
        connector -> connector.setAttribute("maxSwallowSize", -1)
    );
}
```

## Production Reality

Start with properties — they cover 90% of customization needs. Use `WebServerFactoryCustomizer` only for the remaining 10%: custom connector setup, SSL certificate provisioning from a secrets manager, adding request/response filters at the Tomcat level. Programmatic configuration is harder to understand at a glance than a properties file.

## Basic SSL Configuration

```properties
server.port=8443

# PKCS12 keystore
server.ssl.key-store=classpath:keystore.p12
server.ssl.key-store-password=${KEYSTORE_PASSWORD}
server.ssl.key-store-type=PKCS12
server.ssl.key-alias=myapp

# Minimum TLS version
server.ssl.enabled-protocols=TLSv1.2,TLSv1.3

# Cipher suites (restrict to strong ones)
server.ssl.ciphers=TLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256,TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384
```

## Generating a Self-Signed Certificate (Dev Only)

```bash
keytool -genkeypair \
    -alias myapp \
    -keyalg RSA \
    -keysize 2048 \
    -storetype PKCS12 \
    -keystore keystore.p12 \
    -validity 365 \
    -storepass changeit
```

## Redirect HTTP to HTTPS

To keep both HTTP (redirect) and HTTPS (traffic), add a second connector:

```java
@Bean
public WebServerFactoryCustomizer<TomcatServletWebServerFactory> httpsRedirect() {
    return factory -> {
        factory.addAdditionalTomcatConnectors(createHttpConnector());
    };
}

private Connector createHttpConnector() {
    Connector connector = new Connector("org.apache.coyote.http11.Http11NioProtocol");
    connector.setPort(8080);
    connector.setRedirectPort(8443);
    return connector;
}
```

## Production Reality

For production, terminate TLS at the infrastructure layer (AWS ALB, GCP Load Balancer, Nginx, or Kubernetes ingress). Your application communicates plain HTTP internally. This approach:
- Simplifies certificate management (Let's Encrypt auto-renewal at the LB level)
- Offloads TLS computation to hardware-optimized load balancers
- Allows certificate rotation without application restart

## Factory Hierarchy

```
WebServerFactory
└── ConfigurableWebServerFactory      (port, address, SSL, compression)
    └── ConfigurableServletWebServerFactory  (context path, session config)
        ├── TomcatServletWebServerFactory
        ├── UndertowServletWebServerFactory
        └── JettyServletWebServerFactory
```

## Customizer Approach (Preferred)

```java
@Component
public class MyServerCustomizer 
    implements WebServerFactoryCustomizer<TomcatServletWebServerFactory> {

    @Override
    public void customize(TomcatServletWebServerFactory factory) {
        factory.addConnectorCustomizers(connector -> {
            Http11NioProtocol protocol = (Http11NioProtocol) connector.getProtocolHandler();
            protocol.setMaxConnections(20000);
            protocol.setMaxThreads(400);
        });
    }
}
```

## Full Factory Override (Advanced)

```java
@Bean
public TomcatServletWebServerFactory tomcatFactory() {
    return new TomcatServletWebServerFactory() {
        @Override
        protected void postProcessContext(Context context) {
            // Deep context customization
            context.setManager(new PersistentManager());
        }
    };
}
```

## Production Reality

The factory override approach breaks auto-configuration for that factory — any customizer beans targeting the same factory type still apply, but the auto-configured defaults you previously relied on are now your responsibility to set. Start with `@ConfigurationProperties`-bound properties, then `WebServerFactoryCustomizer`, and only resort to a full factory override as a last resort.

## Configuration

```properties
# Enable multipart (default: true)
spring.servlet.multipart.enabled=true

# Max single file size (default: 1MB)
spring.servlet.multipart.max-file-size=10MB

# Max total request size (default: 10MB)
spring.servlet.multipart.max-request-size=50MB

# Threshold above which files are written to disk (default: 0)
spring.servlet.multipart.file-size-threshold=2KB

# Temp directory for multipart files
spring.servlet.multipart.location=/tmp
```

## Controller

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/files")
public class FileUploadController {

    @PostMapping("/upload")
    public ResponseEntity<String> upload(
            @RequestParam("file") MultipartFile file) {

        if (file.isEmpty()) {
            return ResponseEntity.badRequest().body("File is empty");
        }

        // Good: stream directly, don't load into memory
        try (InputStream in = file.getInputStream()) {
            storageService.store(in, file.getOriginalFilename(), file.getSize());
        }

        return ResponseEntity.ok("Uploaded: " + file.getOriginalFilename());
    }

    @PostMapping("/upload-multiple")
    public ResponseEntity<List<String>> uploadMultiple(
            @RequestParam("files") List<MultipartFile> files) {
        return ResponseEntity.ok(
            files.stream()
                .map(MultipartFile::getOriginalFilename)
                .collect(Collectors.toList())
        );
    }
}
```

## Production Reality

For large file uploads, consider pre-signed upload URLs (S3 pre-signed URLs, GCS signed URLs). The client uploads directly to object storage — your application server never sees the file content. This removes the file size limit concern, eliminates memory pressure, and is faster since the client uploads to geographically closer storage endpoints.
